<article class="guide">
  <header class="guide-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Read this first</p>
    <h1>What KFOS is, and how we run it</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      KFOS is Keverd's operating system for a three-person, pre-revenue team.
      It is not a task manager, and it isn't here to prove anyone sat at a
      desk for six hours. It exists to make one thing visible every day:
      did this move the company forward, and where's the evidence?
    </p>
  </header>

  <section>
    <h2>Why this exists</h2>
    <p>
      We're pre-revenue with three founders and a mandate to put in real
      hours every day. A task list rewards whoever finishes the most small,
      easy things — not whoever moved the company. So instead of counting
      tasks, KFOS tracks <strong>evidence against a fixed priority order</strong>:
    </p>
    <ol class="priority-list">
      <li><strong>Revenue</strong> — does this help us acquire, retain, or get paid by a customer?</li>
      <li><strong>Product</strong> — does this make Keverd materially better?</li>
      <li><strong>Distribution</strong> — does this increase our ability to reach customers?</li>
      <li><strong>Infrastructure</strong> — does this make the company faster, safer, or more scalable?</li>
      <li><strong>Learning</strong> — does this give us information that changes a decision?</li>
      <li class="muted">Everything else — probably don't do it.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      When two pieces of work compete for the same hour, the one serving the
      higher pillar on this list wins. That's the whole decision rule.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The five pillars</h2>
    <div class="pillar-grid">
      <% PILLARS.forEach(p => { %>
        <div class="pillar-card">
          <div class="pillar-card-head"><span class="ico"><%= p.emoji %></span> <%= p.label %></div>
          <p class="small muted"><%= p.question %></p>
        </div>
      <% }) %>
    </div>
    <p class="small muted">
      A sixth slot, <span class="ico"><%= PRIORITY_PSEUDO_PILLAR.emoji %></span> <strong>Founder Priority</strong>,
      runs on Thursdays: whatever is the single most important problem right
      now, even if it doesn't sit neatly in one pillar.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How the week runs</h2>
    <table class="guide-table">
      <tr><th>Day</th><th>Pillar</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Mon</td><td><span class="ico">💰</span> Revenue</td><td>Session on pipeline, customers, pricing, objections.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tue</td><td><span class="ico">🧠</span> Product</td><td>Session on what's weak, what's blocking adoption, what to build or not build.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Wed</td><td><span class="ico">📣</span> Distribution</td><td>Session on how people discover Keverd — outbound, content, partnerships.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Thu</td><td><span class="ico">🎯</span> Founder Priority</td><td>Whatever matters most this week, decided by that day's owner.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fri</td><td><span class="ico">🔬</span> Learning + Review</td><td>Close the week: what moved, what didn't, what we learned, what changes.</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p>
      Each session has an <strong>owner</strong> — the founder responsible for
      that day's conversation. The pillar stays fixed to the weekday, but
      ownership rotates fairly across the three of us, so nobody permanently
      becomes "the sales person" or "the coding person." KFOS computes this
      rotation automatically (see Settings for the schedule).
    </p>
    <p>
      Owning a session means five things: come with a real objective, keep
      the conversation from running in circles, force it into a decision,
      write the resolution down, and follow up on it next time that pillar
      comes around. The owner isn't personally on the hook for every
      resulting task — they're on the hook for the conversation producing a
      decision with an owner.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The rules</h2>
    <ul class="rules-list">
      <li>No commitment without an owner.</li>
      <li>No completion without evidence.</li>
      <li>No failure without learning.</li>
      <li>If it isn't on the Commitments board, it isn't an official company commitment.</li>
      <li>If you spend time on something, it must move one of the five pillars forward — if it doesn't, question why we're doing it.</li>
      <li>Every working day produces evidence: something shipped, someone contacted, something validated, something learned, or a decision made.</li>
      <li>We measure outcomes, not task counts.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What counts as evidence</h2>
    <p class="small muted">"I was thinking about the product for 3 hours" isn't evidence. "I investigated why X happens, tested three approaches, rejected two, and implemented Y" is. Evidence falls into five categories:</p>
    <dl class="evidence-list">
      <dt>Shipped</dt><dd>Something changed — a PR, a feature, a deployment, a document, a campaign.</dd>
      <dt>Contacted</dt><dd>Someone outside Keverd was engaged — a prospect, partner, customer, expert.</dd>
      <dt>Learned</dt><dd>You discovered something, with a conclusion attached — not "researched X," but "X means we should do Y."</dd>
      <dt>Validated</dt><dd>You tested an assumption against reality and got a real answer.</dd>
      <dt>Decided</dt><dd>A meaningful decision was made based on evidence, and why.</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The six hours</h2>
    <p>
      The requirement is <strong><%= dailyHourTarget %> hours of documented
      productive contribution</strong> per working day — not six hours of
      sitting at a desk. Two hours on customer calls, two hours building,
      one hour of research, one hour documenting counts. So does five hours
      on a major feature and one hour testing it. The Daily Founder Log
      takes 5–10 minutes: hours plus evidence per pillar, biggest outcome,
      biggest problem, and a decision for tomorrow.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Reading the scorecard and weekly score</h2>
    <p class="small muted">
      Commitments resolve to one of four states: <strong>done</strong> (evidence
      exists), <strong>progressing</strong> (real work happened, not finished),
      <strong>missed</strong> (it wasn't delivered), or <strong>cancelled</strong>
      (deliberately dropped, with a reason — that's good startup behavior, not
      failure, as long as it's explicit rather than something that quietly
      disappeared). The weekly score on each pillar rolls this up. A founder
      repeatedly missing commitments isn't automatically a problem — it's a
      signal to investigate why: unclear priorities, an unrealistic
      commitment, a real blocker, or a role not being fulfilled.
    </p>
  </section>

